{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_09-22-37-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && wc -c update.json && python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('update.json'))\nprint(list(d.keys()))\nprint(json.dumps({k:v for k,v in d.items() if k\"'!='\"'questions'}, indent=1)[:3000])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"68477 update.json\n['rationale', 'sources', 'investigations', 'gaps', 'interpretations', 'knowledge']\n{\n \"rationale\": \"Trace how a restarted node re-establishes its configuration ('context validity') when the committed-log replay feature is enabled. NewRaft replays committed logs first (restoreFromCommittedLogs sets lastApplied to the persisted commit index) and then scans the log for configuration entries starting at max(lastSnapshot, lastApplied)+1. Because the scan is skipped over the committed prefix, configuration entries inside it are never applied to r.configurations when no snapshot carries them, even though they remain in the log. Focused probe: restart a single-voter node that was bootstrapped and applied commands but never snapshotted, with RestoreCommittedLogs on/off and with a covering snapshot as control. Reproducibility question: the dual-majority outcome depends on the ordering between the partitioned side's election and the stale leader's lease, so its successor experiment is repeated once from its retained inputs in a clean copy to see whether the concurrent-leader window and the truncation of the committed entry recur under schedule variation. Reproducibility question: the corrected resurrected-voter experiment is repeated once from its retained inputs in a clean copy, because the premature-commit observation depends on the stale leader winning its re-election after the restart and on the removed voter having caught up before the partition. Reproducibility question: the forgotten-added-voter experiment is repeated once from its retained inputs in a clean copy, since the premature commit depends on the restarted leader winning its re-election with an understated quorum and on the partition leaving exactly two connected voters. Reproducibility question: the three-voter simultaneous-restart experiment is repeated once from its retained inputs in a clean copy, because the progress claim (no leader while every voter holds the configuration only in its log) is the headline liveness consequence and currently rests on a single scored execution with its control. Reproducibility question: the original restart probe (whose receipt is the most cited witness of the empty-configuration case, including its own non-replay and snapshot controls in one execution) is repeated once from its retained inputs in a clean copy, to confirm that the primary witness and both in-harness controls reproduce together. Reproducibility question: the partial-restart experiment is repeated once from its retained inputs in a clean copy, because that harness contains the timing-dependent half of the progress claim (which node leads after two of three voters restart) as well as the structural half (a live quorum that cannot elect once the configured voter stops). Correction of the repeat target: the repeat just executed (receipt 8f0b76d0cd49441c81ecb603f1fcd527) ran the superseded revision of the partial-restart experiment (experiment a8b8d696ac934beda1434a93b5d7758f-experiment), whose captured harness still used helpers declared in a sibling file and therefore fail\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
